<Group view="presentation" inline-output="false" labelreference="L170" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Above are plots of the Mean Anomaly differences in cartesian OEs and the LPE equations overlayed with eachother. My intuition tells me that error growth and numerical stability is what is going to drive differences between these two graphics. Cartesian coordinates does a better job of handling singularities than the LPEs do, which could be driving numerical error in the graphics above. Additionally, error can grow in the LPEs faster than cartesian coordinates, which is shown by the magnitude of the blue line reaching higher peaks than the red, because of the dense computations that go into integrating the LPEs. The absurd amount of partials and computations involved make error much more of a sensitive metric compared to cartesian. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
